PAGES=[page('090_page_1.jpg',171,[heading('Example 16 (c) — solution continued'),eq(r'\left[\frac{x^3}3+x\right]_0^2=-\left[\frac{x^3}3+x\right]_2^0\Rightarrow\frac83+2=-[0-(\frac83+2)]\Rightarrow\frac{14}3=\frac{14}3'),eq(r'\mathrm{d.}\quad\int_0^2(x^2+1)\,dx=\int_0^1(x^2+1)\,dx+\int_1^2(x^2+1)\,dx'),eq(r'\left[\frac{x^3}3+x\right]_0^2=\left[\frac{x^3}3+x\right]_0^1+\left[\frac{x^3}3+x\right]_1^2'),eq(r'\frac{14}3=\frac43+\frac83+2-(\frac13+1)\Rightarrow\frac{14}3=\frac{14}3'),eq(r'\mathrm{e.}\quad\int_{-1}^1x^2\,dx=\int_{-1}^0x^2\,dx+\int_0^1x^2\,dx=2\int_0^1x^2\,dx'),eq(r'\left[\frac{x^3}3\right]_{-1}^1=\left[\frac{x^3}3\right]_{-1}^0+\left[\frac{x^3}3\right]_0^1=2\left[\frac{x^3}3\right]_0^1'),eq(r'\frac13+\frac13=0+\frac13+\frac13=2(\frac13)\Rightarrow\frac23=\frac23=\frac23'),heading('Example 17'),paragraph(r'Evaluate (a) \(\int_0^1\frac{2e^{4x}-3}{e^{2x}}\,dx\); (b) \(\int_{\pi/3}^{\pi/2}x\sin x^2\,dx\).'),heading('Solution'),eq(r'\mathrm{a.}\quad\int_0^1\frac{2e^{4x}-3}{e^{2x}}\,dx=\int_0^1(2e^{2x}-3e^{-2x})\,dx'),eq(r'=\left[2\frac{e^{2x}}2-3\frac{e^{-2x}}{-2}\right]_0^1=\left[e^{2x}+\frac32e^{-2x}\right]_0^1'),eq(r'=(e^2+\frac32e^{-2})-(e^0+\frac32e^0)=e^2+\frac32e^{-2}-\frac52=5.092'),paragraph('b. We need to substitute a new variable u(x):'),eq(r'x^2=u,\quad\frac d{dx}(x^2)=\frac{du}{dx}\Rightarrow2x=\frac{du}{dx}\Rightarrow x\,dx=\frac{du}2'),paragraph('The lower and upper limit of x = π/3 and x = π/2 are used in x² = u to obtain the lower and upper limit of u:'),eq(r'x=\frac\pi3\Rightarrow u=(\frac\pi3)^2=\frac{\pi^2}9'),eq(r'x=\frac\pi2\Rightarrow u=(\frac\pi2)^2=\frac{\pi^2}4'),paragraph('Substitute all these in the given integral to obtain:'),eq(r'\int_{\pi/3}^{\pi/2}x\sin x^2\,dx=\frac12\int_{\pi^2/9}^{\pi^2/4}\sin u\,du=\left[-\frac{\cos u}2\right]_{\pi^2/9}^{\pi^2/4}'),eq(r'=-\frac12(\cos\frac{\pi^2}4-\cos\frac{\pi^2}9)=-\frac12[\cos(2.4647)-\cos(1.0966)]'),eq(r'=-\frac12(-0.7812-0.4566)=-\frac12(-1.2378)=0.6189'),note('The source rounds π²/4 as 2.4647 instead of approximately 2.467401100. Using the exact endpoints gives 0.6189076428, which still rounds to its final 0.6189. The expression sin x² here means sin(x²), as confirmed by the source substitution.'),heading('Example 18'),paragraph(r'Evaluate (a) \(\int_0^1xe^x\,dx\); (b) \(\int_0^1e^x\sin x\,dx\).'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('a. The technique of integration by parts with u = x and dv/dx = eˣ is used to obtain:'),eq(r'\int_0^1xe^x\,dx=[xe^x]_0^1-\int_0^1e^x\,dx,\quad u=x,\quad du=dx,\quad\frac{dv}{dx}=e^x')
]),page('090_page_2.jpg',172,[heading('Example 18 (a) — solution continued'),eq(r'=(e^1-0)-[e^x]_0^1=(e^1-0)-(e^1-e^0)=e^1-e^1+e^0=1'),heading('b.'),paragraph('The integral is'),eq(r'I=\int_0^1e^x\sin x\,dx'),paragraph('The integration by parts rule with substitution u = eˣ and dv/dx = sin x is used to obtain:'),eq(r'I=\int_0^1e^x\sin x\,dx=[e^x(-\cos x)]_0^1-\int_0^1(-\cos x)e^x\,dx'),eq(r'u=e^x,\quad du=e^x\,dx,\quad\frac{dv}{dx}=\sin x,\quad v=-\cos x'),eq(r'=-(e^1\cos1-e^0\cos0)+\int_0^1e^x\cos x\,dx'),eq(r'=-2.718(0.540)+1+\int_0^1e^x\cos x\,dx=-0.468+\int_0^1e^x\cos x\,dx'),paragraph('Use radians. Again integration by parts'),eq(r'=-0.468+[e^x\sin x]_0^1-\int_0^1(\sin x)e^x\,dx'),eq(r'u=e^x,\quad\frac{dv}{dx}=\cos x,\quad v=\sin x'),eq(r'=-0.468+(e^1\sin1-e^0\sin0)-\int_0^1e^x\sin x\,dx'),eq(r'I=-0.468+[2.718(0.841)-(1)(0)]-I'),eq(r'2I=-0.468+2.287=1.819'),eq(r'I=\frac{1.819}2=0.91'),note('These are rounded source intermediates. The exact answer is [e(sin 1−cos 1)+1]/2 ≈ 0.9093306736, in radians.'),heading('6.6.4 Definite integral as the area under the curve'),paragraph('If f(x) is continuous and f(x) ≥ 0 on the closed interval [a,b], then the area under a curve y = f(x) on the interval [a,b] is given by the definite integral of the function f(x) on [a,b]:'),eq(r'\mathrm{Area}=\int_a^bf(x)\,dx=F(b)-F(a)','i'),heading('Area between a curve and the x-axis'),paragraph('The steps involved in finding the area between a curve and the x-axis are the following:'),paragraph('i. The definite integral ∫ₐᵇf(x)dx presents the sum of the signed area between the graph of y = f(x) and the x-axis from x = a to x = b, where the area above the x-axis (peak) are counted positively and the area below the x-axis (valley) are counted negatively. This is shown in the Figure 6.6.'),fig(6,'signed','negative region A before c and positive region B after c')])]
