EXAMPLES=[]
def ex(n,q,steps,answer,pages):
 EXAMPLES.append({'id':'we-6-source-'+str(n),'num':str(n),'title':'Example '+str(n),'problem':q,'given':'The original definite integrands and endpoint values.','method':'Use endpoint antiderivative values and the printed integral properties.','steps':steps,'answer':answer,'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':min(pages),'pageEnd':max(pages),'verification':'all source questions, limits and worked results checked'}]})
ex(16,r'Evaluate the source properties: (a) \(\int_1^2(2x^2+4x+1)dx\); (b) the same integral with variable y; (c) \(\int_0^2(x^2+1)dx=-\int_2^0(x^2+1)dx\); (d) split that integral at 1; (e) \(\int_{-1}^1x^2dx=2\int_0^1x^2dx\).',['(a) An antiderivative of 2x²+4x+1 is 2x³/3+2x²+x.','At 2 this is 46/3, and at 1 it is 11/3; subtract to get 35/3.','(b) Renaming the integration variable to y changes no numerical value, so the result is again 35/3.','(c) For x²+1, use H(x) = x³/3+x. H(2)−H(0) = 14/3; the reverse integral is −14/3 and its negative is 14/3.','(d) From 0 to 1, the value is H(1)−H(0) = 4/3. From 1 to 2, it is H(2)−H(1) = 10/3.','Add the two pieces: 4/3+10/3 = 14/3, the whole-interval result.','(e) x² is even; ∫₀¹x²dx = 1/3, so the symmetric integral is twice that: 2/3. This also equals [x³/3] from −1 to 1.'],'(a),(b) 35/3; (c),(d) 14/3; (e) 2/3.',[170,171])
ex(17,r'Evaluate (a) \(\int_0^1(2e^{4x}-3)/e^{2x}\,dx\); (b) \(\int_{\pi/3}^{\pi/2}x\sin(x^2)\,dx\).',['(a) Divide each numerator term by e²ˣ: the integrand is 2e²ˣ−3e⁻²ˣ.','A primitive is e²ˣ+(3/2)e⁻²ˣ.','At 1 it gives e²+(3/2)e⁻²; at 0 it gives 1+3/2 = 5/2.','Subtract to obtain e²+(3/2)e⁻²−5/2 ≈ 5.092059024.','(b) Put u = x², du = 2x dx. The new endpoints are π²/9 and π²/4.','The integral becomes (1/2)∫sin u du = [−cos u/2] at those endpoints.','Thus (b) = [cos(π²/9)−cos(π²/4)]/2 ≈ 0.618907643. Use exact endpoints and radians; changing variables requires changing both limits.'],'(a) e²+(3/2)e⁻²−5/2 ≈ 5.092059024; (b) [cos(π²/9)−cos(π²/4)]/2 ≈ 0.618907643.',[171])
ex(18,r'Evaluate (a) \(\int_0^1xe^x\,dx\); (b) \(\int_0^1e^x\sin x\,dx\).',['(a) Integration by parts with u = x,dv = eˣdx gives the primitive eˣ(x−1).','At 1 this is 0; at 0 it is −1, so the definite value is 1.','(b) Let I = ∫₀¹eˣsin x dx and J = ∫₀¹eˣcos x dx.','Parts gives I = [−eˣcos x]₀¹+J = 1−e cos 1+J.','A second application gives J = [eˣsin x]₀¹−I = e sin 1−I.','Substitute: I = 1−e cos 1+e sin 1−I, so 2I = 1+e(sin 1−cos 1).','Therefore I = [1+e(sin 1−cos 1)]/2 ≈ 0.909330674, which rounds to the source 0.91. Keep full precision until the final rounding.'],'(a) 1; (b) [1+e(sin 1−cos 1)]/2 ≈ 0.909330674.',[171,172])
